\begin{abstract}
We study admissions with one open category and several reserve categories when students may qualify for more than one reserve category at once. Under the one-to-one counting rule an admitted student is counted toward at most one category, so a mechanism must decide not only who is admitted but which category each overlapping student fills. We additionally impose a score constraint bounding the gap between the open cutoff and each reserve cutoff. Non-wastefulness must be restated relative to it; within-category fairness need not be, since an upward within-category replacement cannot destroy score-feasibility. A student-proposing score-elevated mechanism (SPSE) is feasible, non-wasteful, strategy-proof and free of justified envy, but need not be score-feasible. Score-elevated policies breach cutoff rules already without overlap, so we isolate what overlap adds: the routing of a multiply eligible student. We then characterize when score-feasibility, open-completeness and reserve-completeness are jointly attainable by a Hall-type condition, and show they are jointly unattainable \emph{even when every reserve category is individually fillable score-feasibly}, the obstruction being a failure of that condition on non-singleton sets, which cannot occur without overlap. Finally, a preference-aware overlapping de-reservation mechanism (PA-ODR) treats score-feasibility as hard and reallocates unfulfillable reserve capacity. It restores score-feasibility and retains unweakened within-category fairness, but forfeits strategy-proofness and no justified envy, and de-reserves an order-dependent, non-minimal amount.

\keywords{Matching \and Affirmative action \and Reserve categories \and Overlapping types \and De-reservation \and School choice}
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
Reserve-based admissions and controlled school choice are studied extensively \cite{Abdu05a,EHYY14a,HYY13a,EcYe15a,AyTu20a,AyTu23a}. In standard formulations each student belongs to a single protected category or none; in many real systems, however, categories overlap. S\"onmez and Yenmez study overlapping reserves \cite{SoYe20a} and the vertical--horizontal system of Indian affirmative action \cite{SoYe22a}; related overlapping-type environments appear in \cite{KHIY17a,AyBo21a,DKT23a} and, more broadly, under distributional constraints in \cite{GKK+17a,KaKo24a}. Indian engineering admissions, the setting closest to ours, are described in \cite{BCC+19b}. That \emph{which} slot a student is counted against is itself a design variable is made explicit by Kominers and S\"onmez \cite{KoSo16a}, and the cost of getting that accounting wrong is documented in \cite{DKPS18a}. The order in which reserve categories are processed is likewise consequential rather than procedural, as Pathak, S\"onmez, \"Unver and Yenmez \cite{PSUY24a} and Pathak, Rees-Jones and S\"onmez \cite{PRS23a} show; the same sensitivity reappears here in Example~\ref{ex:order}.

Once categories overlap, a mechanism must decide not only which students are admitted but how overlapping students are counted. We add a second ingredient, a score constraint keeping the open cutoff and each reserve cutoff close. This is not a stylized device: in \emph{Ashoka Kumar Thakur vs.\ Union of India} the Supreme Court of India recommended a maximum ten-point difference between the open and OBC cutoffs, and Ayg\"un and Turhan \cite{AyTu23b} analyze that directive; cutoff-based concerns appear more generally in \cite{AyTu23a,ABB24a}.

\subsubsection{Why these two ingredients, informally.}
A reader who does not work in matching theory may reasonably ask why either ingredient needs a theory at all. Consider a school with one open seat and two reserve seats, one for each of two disadvantaged groups, and a student who belongs to both groups. She can be counted only once, so admitting her leaves one of the two reserve seats to somebody else, and \emph{which} of the two she is counted against decides who that somebody is. A rule that simply hands her the category she likes best is therefore already making a distributional decision on behalf of students who had no choice of category at all. This is the sense in which the counting rule, and not only the admission rule, is the object being designed; that the slot a student is counted against is a design variable is the point made by Kominers and S\"onmez \cite{KoSo16a}, and \cite{DKPS18a} documents what getting it wrong costs in practice.

The second ingredient says that a reserve seat may not be sold too cheaply: the cutoff-gap rule caps how far below the open cutoff a reserve admit may fall, so that a reserve category remains an alternative route into a selective school rather than a separate and much easier one. Putting the two together produces the effect this paper is about. A reserve seat can be nominally free, with an eligible student waiting for it, and still be unusable, because the only students left eligible for it would push that category's cutoff below the legal bound. Something must then give, and our two mechanisms give up opposite things: SPSE honours the announced quota and accepts a cutoff gap the rule forbids, while PA-ODR honours the rule and retires the quota.

\subsubsection{Relation to the closest prior work.}
S\"onmez and Yenmez \cite{SoYe20a,SoYe22a} take reserve capacities as given and impose no cutoff-gap requirement, the constraint driving every negative result here. Ayg\"un and Turhan \cite{AyTu23b} study exactly the score-elevated policy and the ten-point rule, but without overlapping eligibility, so their mechanism never decides \emph{which} category a student fills. We should therefore be explicit about what is new here. That a score-elevated mechanism can breach a cutoff-gap rule is \emph{not} new and needs no overlap; it is their inconsistency result. What overlap adds is a second and logically independent cause, described in item~2 below and isolated in Remark~\ref{rem:overlap-cause}: when a student is eligible for several categories but counted once, the routing decision itself decides whether the cutoff rule survives, and a routing optimal for her can destroy a score-feasibility another routing preserves. The de-reservation response is in the spirit of \cite{AyTu23a}.

\subsubsection{Contributions.}

