The paper has five contributions, of which the fourth is the main one: a new algorithm that uses de-reservation to enforce the score constraint. The first three build up to it, by adapting the properties to this setting, showing that the natural modification of deferred acceptance does not satisfy the score constraint, and proving that no mechanism holding capacities fixed can. Table~\ref{tab:comparison} summarizes what the two mechanisms do and do not deliver; every entry is defined and proved where the table indicates.

\begin{enumerate}
\item \emph{Properties adapted to this setting.} We formalize admissions with overlapping reserve categories under a common score bonus and a cutoff-gap constraint, and adapt the standard desiderata \cite{EHYY14a,KHIY17a,GKK+17a} to it (Section~\ref{sec:model}). The constraint does not affect them uniformly, and saying which ones it changes is part of the contribution. Non-wastefulness must be restated relative to it, since a seat that no remaining student could legally occupy is not really an empty seat. Within-category fairness needs no such restatement, because promoting a higher-scoring eligible student inside a category can only raise that category's cutoff and hence cannot destroy score-feasibility (Lemma~\ref{lem:fair-sf}). The fairness notion we then use is therefore not an artificially weakened one. Full-capacity commitments are stated separately as open- and reserve-completeness (Definition~\ref{def:complete}), which are strictly stronger than non-wastefulness: non-wastefulness only forbids leaving a seat that somebody could legally take, whereas completeness demands that the seat actually be taken, and for the open category taken by the highest scorers.
\item \emph{A modified deferred-acceptance mechanism does not guarantee score-feasibility.} We modify student-proposing deferred acceptance to run over categories with score-elevated reserve rankings, giving SPSE, and show it is feasible, non-wasteful, fair within categories, free of justified envy and strategy-proof, yet need not be score-feasible (Section~\ref{sec:spse}). We then isolate what overlap contributes to that failure. One cause is already known and needs no overlap: the eligible pool may simply be too weak, which is the inconsistency of \cite{AyTu23b}. The cause specific to our setting is that a multiply eligible student can be routed to any of her categories, and the routing alone can decide whether the cutoff rule survives, even when the pool is strong enough for some routing to satisfy it (Proposition~\ref{prop:spse-not-sf}, Remark~\ref{rem:overlap-cause}).
\item \emph{Impossibility result.} The failure above is not a defect of SPSE in particular. Score-feasibility, open-completeness and reserve-completeness are jointly unattainable, and this happens \emph{even on instances where every reserve quota is individually fillable score-feasibly}, so no shortage of qualified candidates is responsible (Definition~\ref{def:indcomp}, Proposition~\ref{prop:impossibility}). The obstruction is the failure of a capacitated Hall condition on non-singleton sets of categories (Remark~\ref{rem:hall}), which cannot occur without overlap. Since the statement quantifies over all mechanisms holding capacities fixed, no better mechanism of that kind exists, and capacity must be allowed to move --- which is what the next contribution does.
\item \emph{Main contribution: a new algorithm based on de-reservation.} We propose PA-ODR (Section~\ref{sec:paodr}), which treats score-feasibility as a hard institutional constraint and de-reserves the reserve capacity it cannot legally use, converting it into open capacity. It is therefore a fallback mechanism rather than a quota-preserving one, and we present it as such. Its output is feasible, score-feasible, non-wasteful relative to the capacities it ends with, and fair within categories in the same unweakened sense as SPSE, and it runs in polynomial time: the reserve side is a single maximum-weight bipartite matching realizing four lexicographic criteria, of which the last two use the students' own preferences to decide which of her eligible categories an overlapping student fills (Remark~\ref{rem:computation}). The prices it pays are the ones Proposition~\ref{prop:impossibility} says some price must be paid on. It de-reserves an order-dependent and non-minimal number of seats (Example~\ref{ex:order}), although minimum de-reservation is itself tractable for boundedly many categories, which locates the non-minimality in the greedy schedule rather than in the objective (Proposition~\ref{prop:minderes}); and it loses no justified envy and strategy-proofness (Example~\ref{ex:paodr-envy}, Proposition~\ref{prop:paodr-notsp}).
\item \emph{Simulations.} We implement both mechanisms and run them on randomly generated instances, sweeping the strictness $\delta$ of the cutoff rule, the one parameter a court or ministry actually sets (Section~\ref{sec:sim}). The runs mirror the theory on data nobody chose adversarially and put numbers on the trade-off: SPSE keeps every quota but satisfies the score rule on as few as $2$ of $20$ instances when the rule is strict, PA-ODR satisfies it always, and the reserve capacity this costs falls monotonically as the rule is relaxed, from $5.75$ of $8.25$ seats to $0.10$. We report this as a sanity check on the implementation and a first quantification, not as an average-case claim.
\end{enumerate}

