\section{Model}
\label{sec:model}

\subsection{Primitives, Counting, Scores and Cutoffs}

We consider admissions to a single school. Let $I$ be a finite set of students, $T$ a finite set of reserve categories, $o$ the open category, and $V=T\cup\{o\}$. Each $c\in V$ has capacity $q^c\in\mathbb{N}$; each $i\in I$ has a raw score $\sigma(i)\in\R_+$, distinct across students, and a reserve-eligibility set $\T(i)\subseteq T$, with $i$ \emph{overlapping} when $|\T(i)|\ge2$. All reserve categories share a common bonus $k\ge0$, and $\delta\ge0$ is the allowed gap between the open and reserve cutoffs. The admissible set of $i$ is $V_i:=\{o\}\cup\T(i)$, over which she has a strict preference $\succ_i$, with $r_i(c)$ the rank of $c$ ($1$ = best). With a single school, students rank admission routes rather than schools, and cannot apply where they lack eligibility.

We adopt \emph{one-to-one counting}: a matched student counts toward exactly one category chosen by the mechanism, not toward every category for which she is eligible. This is the convention of \cite{KHIY17a,AyBo21a,GKK+17a} and models systems in which a student qualifying under both a socioeconomic and a geographic criterion may enter by either route but cannot fill both quotas. An allocation is therefore a matching between students and categories.

\begin{definition}[Matching and feasibility]
A matching is a tuple $\mu=(\mu_c)_{c\in V}$ with $\mu_c\subseteq I$; write $M(\mu):=\bigcup_{c\in V}\mu_c$ for the matched students. It is \emph{feasible} if (i) $|\mu_c|\le q^c$ for every $c\in V$, (ii) $\mu_c\cap\mu_{c'}=\emptyset$ for distinct $c,c'$, and (iii) $c\in V_i$ for every $i\in\mu_c$.
\end{definition}

Condition (ii) is the formal statement of one-to-one counting. The open category ranks by raw score, reserve categories by score plus the common bonus. As $i$ is assignable only within $V_i$, define for $c\in V_i$ the \emph{adjusted score} $\sigma_c(i):=\sigma(i)$ if $c=o$ and $\sigma_c(i):=\sigma(i)+k$ if $c=t\in\T(i)$. Reserve categories thus share one ranking over their eligible students, differing only through eligibility, capacities and preferences. The case $k=0$, in which every category ranks by raw score, is covered throughout.

\begin{definition}[Cutoffs and score-feasibility]
\label{def:scorefeas}
For a matching $\mu$ let $e_o(\mu):=\min_{i\in\mu_o}\sigma(i)$, with $e_o(\mu)=-\infty$ if $\mu_o=\emptyset$, and for $t\in T$ let $e_t^+(\mu):=\min_{i\in\mu_t}\sigma_t(i)$, with $e_t^+(\mu)=+\infty$ if $\mu_t=\emptyset$. A feasible $\mu$ is \emph{score-feasible} if $e_o(\mu)-e_t^+(\mu)\le\delta$ for every $t\in T$.
\end{definition}

We read $e_o(\mu)-e_t^+(\mu)$ as $-\infty$ whenever $\mu_o=\emptyset$ or $\mu_t=\emptyset$, including when both are, so the condition is vacuous there and needs no side condition. No reserve cutoff may fall too far below the open one, measured on the adjusted scale: the bonus is institutionally legitimate and $\delta$ bounds only the gap left after it. The two parameters thus do different work rather than granting the advantage twice, though in raw scores they compound, a reserve admit falling up to $\delta+k$ below the open cutoff; for $k=0$ they coincide and $\delta$ alone bounds the raw gap, which is the ten-point rule of \cite{AyTu23b}. Score-feasibility is an ex post requirement on realized cutoffs, not something implied by the within-category ranking rule, which is why a second mechanism is needed in Section~\ref{sec:paodr}.

\subsection{Desiderata}

Wastefulness is usually defined by an empty seat an unmatched student could occupy. Here that is not enough: she can occupy it only when score-feasibility is preserved.

\begin{definition}[Non-wastefulness]
\label{def:nw}
A feasible matching $\mu$ is \emph{non-wasteful} if there are no unmatched $i\in I\setminus M(\mu)$ and $c\in V_i$ such that adding $i$ to $c$ yields a matching that is both feasible and score-feasible.
\end{definition}

In a non-wasteful matching for our model, a reserve seat may be empty yet unusable. Two readings matter: the definition applies to feasible, not only score-feasible matchings, since SPSE below is non-wasteful without being score-feasible; and it requires the \emph{enlarged} matching to be score-feasible rather than `score-feasibility to be preserved', otherwise an originally score-infeasible matching will making the constraint lose effectiveness and may being vacuously non-wasteful. Definition~\ref{def:nw-hat} reads the same way.

Non-wastefulness is a weak requirement, and the axioms our impossibility uses are the stronger commitments an institution actually announces.

\begin{definition}[Open- and reserve-completeness]
\label{def:complete}
A feasible matching $\mu$ is \emph{open-complete} if $\mu_o$ consists of the $\min\{q^o,|I|\}$ highest raw-score students in $I$, and \emph{reserve-complete} if $|\mu_t|=q^t$ for every $t\in T$ for which at least $q^t$ students are eligible.
\end{definition}

Neither is implied by Definition~\ref{def:nw}, and the difference is exactly the score rule: non-wastefulness forbids leaving a seat that some student could legally take, while completeness insists the seat be taken, and for the open category taken by raw merit with nobody held back as more useful elsewhere. Reserve-completeness is the full-utilization requirement quotas are written to express, counting eligibility category by category precisely because policy documents are drafted without reference to overlap.

\begin{definition}[Within-category fairness]
\label{def:fair}
A feasible matching $\mu$ is \emph{fair within categories} if there are no $t\in T$ and students $i,j$ with (i) $t\in\T(i)\cap\T(j)$, (ii) $\sigma_t(i)>\sigma_t(j)$, (iii) $j\in\mu_t$, and (iv) $i\notin M(\mu)$.
\end{definition}

The definition only ranging over reserve categories is deliberate: this is the minimal notion on which our two mechanisms agree, isolating the guarantee that a reserve seat never holds someone a stronger eligible claimant could displace. Complaints aimed at the open category, and those by already-matched students, belong to Definition~\ref{def:nje}, which is a stronger notion of fairness, but not accompanied by both mechanisms due to necessary tradeoffs.

\begin{lemma}[Within-category fairness needs no score-relative weakening]
\label{lem:fair-sf}
Let $\mu$ be feasible, $t\in T$, $j\in\mu_t$, and $i\notin M(\mu)$ with $t\in\T(i)$ and $\sigma_t(i)>\sigma_t(j)$. Let $\mu'$ replace $j$ by $i$ in $\mu_t$. Then $\mu'$ is feasible, and if $\mu$ is score-feasible so is $\mu'$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Feasibility is immediate: $|\mu'_t|=|\mu_t|\le q^t$, $i$ was assigned nowhere so nobody is counted twice, and $t\in\T(i)\subseteq V_i$. The swap changes no other category and leaves $\mu_o$ untouched, so $e_o(\mu')=e_o(\mu)$ and $e_{t'}^+(\mu')=e_{t'}^+(\mu)$ for $t'\neq t$. For $t$, $\mu'_t=(\mu_t\setminus\{j\})\cup\{i\}$ with $\sigma_t(i)>\sigma_t(j)$ gives $e_t^+(\mu')\ge e_t^+(\mu)$, hence $e_o(\mu')-e_t^+(\mu')\le e_o(\mu)-e_t^+(\mu)\le\delta$.
\end{proof}

The asymmetry with non-wastefulness is genuine: adding a \emph{new} student to a reserve category can lower its cutoff, whereas \emph{replacing} one by a better student cannot, which is why only Definition~\ref{def:nw} carries a score-feasibility clause. The next requirement is the standard no-justified-envy condition transposed to categories; it also lets a \emph{matched} student complain about a category she ranks higher, and admits the open category as an object of complaint.

\begin{definition}[No justified envy]
\label{def:nje}
A feasible matching $\mu$ has \emph{no justified envy} if there are no student $i$ and category $c\in V_i$ with (i) either $i\notin M(\mu)$, or $i\in\mu_{c'}$ for some $c'\in V_i$ with $c\succ_i c'$; and (ii) either $|\mu_c|<q^c$, or some $j\in\mu_c$ has $\sigma_c(j)<\sigma_c(i)$.
\end{definition}

No justified envy implies within-category fairness: if $i\notin M(\mu)$, $j\in\mu_t$ and $\sigma_t(i)>\sigma_t(j)$ with $t\in\T(i)$, then $c=t$ witnesses justified envy. The converse fails by Example~\ref{ex:paodr-envy}.

Unlike Definition~\ref{def:fair}, omitting a score-feasibility clause here is a genuine choice, since Lemma~\ref{lem:fair-sf} does not extend: clause (ii) also lets $i$ claim a \emph{vacant} seat, lowering that category's cutoff, and clause (i) lets a matched student leave $c'$, which for $c'=o$ \emph{raises} the open cutoff and can break score-feasibility elsewhere. A score-relative weakening would thus have content. We use the unweakened notion because it strengthens Proposition~\ref{prop:spse-nje}; the price is that Example~\ref{ex:paodr-envy} must be checked against the weaker reading, which we do there.

A structural circularity underlies everything: feasible reserve assignments depend on the open cutoff, which depends on who is left after reserve assignment. Score-feasibility is thus not a static eligibility condition and greedy quota-filling is unreliable. SPSE keeps capacities fixed and uses deferred-acceptance logic; PA-ODR treats the cutoff rule as hard and de-reserves, following \cite{AyTu23a}.

\section{Student-Proposing Score-Elevated Mechanism}
\label{sec:spse}

\subsubsection{Summary of this section.}
We analyse the natural first candidate: student-proposing deferred acceptance run over categories instead of schools, with each reserve category ranking its eligible students by the elevated score $\sigma+k$ (Algorithm~\ref{alg:spse}). Holding the announced capacities fixed, it inherits what one expects from deferred acceptance. It is feasible and non-wasteful (Proposition~\ref{prop:spse-basic}), has no justified envy and hence is fair within categories (Proposition~\ref{prop:spse-nje}), and is strategy-proof (Proposition~\ref{prop:spse-sp}). What it does not deliver is score-feasibility (Proposition~\ref{prop:spse-not-sf}), and Remark~\ref{rem:overlap-cause} separates the two independent reasons why: the eligible pool may be too weak to fill a quota legally, which is known and needs no overlap, or the pool may be adequate and the mechanism may still fail because it routes a multiply eligible student to the wrong category. Section~\ref{sec:impossibility} then shows that this is not a defect of SPSE in particular: no mechanism keeping capacities fixed can be score-feasible, open-complete and reserve-complete at once (Proposition~\ref{prop:impossibility}), which is what forces the de-reserving mechanism of Section~\ref{sec:paodr}.

\begin{algorithm}[t]
\caption{Student-Proposing Score-Elevated Mechanism (SPSE)}
\label{alg:spse}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\Require $I$, $V=T\cup\{o\}$, capacities $(q^c)_{c\in V}$, preferences $(\succ_i)_{i\in I}$, scores $\sigma$, eligibility $\T(\cdot)$, bonus $k$
\Ensure Matching $\mu=(\mu_c)_{c\in V}$
\State $\mu_c\gets\emptyset$ for all $c\in V$; mark every student unmatched
\State Set $V_i:=\{o\}\cup\T(i)$ and compute $\sigma_c(i)$ for every $i$ and every $c\in V_i$
\While{some unmatched $i$ still has an unapplied category in $V_i$}
    \State $i$ applies to her most preferred unapplied $c\in V_i$
    \State $c$ considers $\mu_c\cup\{i\}$ and keeps at most $q^c$ students with highest $\sigma_c$
    \State Every rejected student becomes unmatched
\EndWhile
\State \Return $\mu$
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

SPSE preserves one-to-one counting since a student is held by at most one category at a time, and differs from ordinary student-proposing deferred acceptance only in proposing to categories and in the reserve-side score elevation. Each student applies to each category of $V_i$ at most once, so the loop runs at most $|I|(|T|+1)$ times and terminates.

\begin{proposition}[Feasibility and non-wastefulness]
\label{prop:spse-basic}
The output of SPSE is feasible and non-wasteful in the sense of Definition~\ref{def:nw}.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proposition}[No justified envy]
\label{prop:spse-nje}
The output of SPSE has no justified envy, and is therefore fair within categories.
\end{proposition}

Raw scores and eligibility are verifiable, so the only object a student can misreport is $\succ_i$.

\begin{proposition}[Strategy-proofness]
\label{prop:spse-sp}
No student can obtain a strictly $\succ_i$-better assignment under SPSE by reporting an ordering over $V_i$ other than $\succ_i$, holding others' reports fixed.
\end{proposition}

Proofs of Propositions~\ref{prop:spse-basic}--\ref{prop:spse-sp} are in Appendix~\ref{app:proofs}; each is the corresponding deferred-acceptance argument transposed to categories, the last by reduction to the college-admissions model of Gale and Shapley \cite{GaSh62a} and the theorem of Dubins and Freedman \cite{DuFr81a} and Roth \cite{Roth82a}. The next two results are the ones whose content is an instance rather than an argument, so we keep them here.

\begin{proposition}[SPSE does not guarantee score-feasibility]
\label{prop:spse-not-sf}
The output of SPSE need not be score-feasible, even when a matching that is simultaneously score-feasible, non-wasteful and fair within categories exists.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
Let $T=\{t_1,t_2\}$, $q^o=q^{t_1}=q^{t_2}=1$, $k=5$, $\delta=10$, and take $i_1(100,\emptyset)$, $i_2(95,\{t_1,t_2\})$, $i_3(82,\{t_2\})$ with $t_1\succ_{i_2}t_2\succ_{i_2}o$ and $t_2\succ_{i_3}o$; so $\sigma_{t_1}(i_2)=\sigma_{t_2}(i_2)=100$ and $\sigma_{t_2}(i_3)=87$. In round~1, $i_1$ applies to $o$, $i_2$ to $t_1$ and $i_3$ to $t_2$; each category receives one applicant and holds her, so SPSE terminates with $\mu_o=\{i_1\}$, $\mu_{t_1}=\{i_2\}$, $\mu_{t_2}=\{i_3\}$. Then $e_o(\mu)-e_{t_2}^+(\mu)=100-87=13>10=\delta$, violating score-feasibility.

Consider instead $\nu_o=\{i_1\}$, $\nu_{t_2}=\{i_2\}$, $\nu_{t_1}=\emptyset$. Here $e_o(\nu)=e_{t_2}^+(\nu)=100$, so the gap is $0$, and the constraint for the empty $t_1$ is vacuous; $\nu$ is score-feasible. It is non-wasteful, since the only unmatched student $i_3$ has $V_{i_3}=\{t_2,o\}$ and both are at capacity; and fair within categories, since the sole member of $t_2$ satisfies $\sigma_{t_2}(i_2)=100>87=\sigma_{t_2}(i_3)$.
\end{proof}

We do not claim $\nu$ dominates: SPSE matches all three students while $\nu$ matches two, and $\nu$ itself has justified envy, as $i_2$ prefers $t_1$ and $|\nu_{t_1}|=0<q^{t_1}$. The proposition establishes that the other listed properties do not force score-feasibility, so score-feasibility is an independent requirement, bought here at the price of a seat---the trade-off Section~\ref{sec:paodr} pays in reserve capacity.

\begin{nremark}[Two causes of score-infeasibility, one of them new]
\label{rem:overlap-cause}
Score-elevated policies can breach a cutoff-gap rule with no overlap at all; this is the inconsistency of Ayg\"un and Turhan \cite{AyTu23b}. Take $T=\{t_1\}$, $q^o=q^{t_1}=1$, $k=5$, $\delta=10$, and $i_1(100,\emptyset)$, $i_2(80,\{t_1\})$ with $t_1\succ_{i_2}o$. SPSE returns $\mu_o=\{i_1\}$, $\mu_{t_1}=\{i_2\}$ and $e_o-e_{t_1}^+=100-85=15>\delta$. Here the sole eligible candidate is simply too weak and no assignment decision is at fault; the matching leaving $t_1$ empty is score-feasible, non-wasteful and fair, so even the bare statement of Proposition~\ref{prop:spse-not-sf} already holds without overlap.

Overlap adds a second, logically independent cause. In the instance of Proposition~\ref{prop:spse-not-sf} both $t_1$ and $t_2$ are acceptable categories for $i_2$, with the same adjusted score in either, so no candidate is too weak: $i_2$ alone would satisfy the cutoff rule in whichever category she filled. SPSE resolves the choice by her own preference, sending her to $t_1$ and leaving $t_2$ to the far weaker $i_3$. Under one-to-one counting it is that routing, not the applicant pool, that breaks the rule, and the same pool admits a score-feasible routing. This cause cannot arise under single eligibility, since then there is nothing to route. The rest of the paper concerns it, and Proposition~\ref{prop:impossibility} further provides an impossibility that excludes the first cause.
\end{nremark}

\subsection{An Impossibility Driven by Overlap}
\label{sec:impossibility}

An impossibility here is easy to obtain for the uninteresting reason just described: in the two-student instance in Remark~\ref{rem:overlap-cause}, open-completeness forces $e_o=100$, nobody clears the bar, and reserve-completeness already fails. We therefore build the negation of that reason into the hypothesis.

\begin{definition}[Individual completability]
\label{def:indcomp}
An instance is \emph{individually completable} if for every $t\in T$ there is a feasible matching that is score-feasible, open-complete in the sense of Definition~\ref{def:complete}, and has $|\mu_t|=q^t$.
\end{definition}

So every announced quota is separately honourable in full, score-feasibly and without disturbing the open category. The impossibility is that these promises cannot be kept together.

\begin{proposition}[Impossibility under full capacity utilization]
\label{prop:impossibility}
There is an individually completable instance (Definition~\ref{def:indcomp}) admitting no feasible matching that is simultaneously score-feasible, open-complete and reserve-complete in the sense of Definition~\ref{def:complete}.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
Let $T=\{t_1,t_2\}$, $q^o=q^{t_1}=q^{t_2}=1$, $k=5$, $\delta=10$, and take $i_1(100,\emptyset)$, $i_2(95,\{t_1,t_2\})$, $i_3(80,\{t_1\})$, $i_4(78,\{t_2\})$. Open-completeness forces $\mu_o=\{i_1\}$, so $e_o(\mu)=100$ and score-feasibility requires $\sigma_t(i)\ge90$ for every reserve assignee. Since $\sigma_{t_1}(i_2)=\sigma_{t_2}(i_2)=100\ge90$ while $\sigma_{t_1}(i_3)=85<90$ and $\sigma_{t_2}(i_4)=83<90$, student $i_2$ is the only one placeable in any reserve category.

The instance is individually completable: for $t_1$ take $\mu_o=\{i_1\}$, $\mu_{t_1}=\{i_2\}$, $\mu_{t_2}=\emptyset$, which is open-complete with $|\mu_{t_1}|=q^{t_1}$ and $e_o-e_{t_1}^+=0\le\delta$, the empty $t_2$ being vacuous; exchanging $t_1$ and $t_2$ handles $t_2$. Yet both quotas cannot be met at once: $t_1$ and $t_2$ have eligible sets $\{i_2,i_3\}$ and $\{i_2,i_4\}$, so reserve-completeness demands $|\mu_{t_1}|=|\mu_{t_2}|=1$, requiring two distinct reserve placements, while one-to-one counting lets $i_2$ count toward at most one. Some reserve category must then admit $i_3$ or $i_4$, breaching score-feasibility.
\end{proof}

Both axioms are institutional commitments rather than normative optima, in the sense discussed after Definition~\ref{def:complete}, and the result is a statement about all mechanisms holding capacities fixed rather than about any particular one. Weakening reserve-completeness to demand only what is \emph{jointly} fillable would restore consistency but concede the point, that overlap silently reduces what a stated quota delivers. PA-ODR makes that concession explicit as de-reservation.

What replaces the shortage-of-candidates explanation is a Hall-type condition: writing $N(S)$ for the students admissible at the open cutoff for some category in $S\subseteq T$, the requirement is $|N(S)|\ge\sum_{t\in S}q^t$, individual completability is exactly this condition on singletons, and the impossibility is that singletons do not imply larger sets. The two coincide when the sets $N(\{t\})$ are pairwise disjoint, so the gap between them is precisely overlap. Remark~\ref{rem:hall} in Appendix~\ref{app:hall} verifies the failure on the instance above; it is also why the mechanism of the next section is built around a bipartite matching rather than category-by-category quota filling.

\section{Preference-Aware Overlapping De-Reservation}
\label{sec:paodr}

\advisor{a brief summary}

\subsubsection{Summary of this section.}
Proposition~\ref{prop:impossibility} rules out keeping capacities fixed, so we now treat score-feasibility as hard and let capacity move. PA-ODR (Algorithm~\ref{alg:paodr}) fills the open category by raw score, assigns reserve seats only along student--category pairs admissible at the induced open cutoff, and whenever some reserve seat cannot be filled converts one such seat into an open seat and repeats. The reserve side is a capacitated bipartite matching selected by four lexicographic criteria (Section~\ref{sec:preflex}); criteria 1--2 determine \emph{who} is admitted and leave no freedom there (Lemma~\ref{lem:matroid}), while criteria 3--4 use the students' own preferences to decide only \emph{which} of her eligible categories an overlapping student fills. The mechanism terminates in polynomial time, and its output is score-feasible, feasible and non-wasteful relative to the capacities it ends with, and fair within categories in the same unweakened sense as SPSE (Propositions~\ref{prop:paodr-basic} and~\ref{prop:paodr-fair}); the proof of the last of these shows that the cutoff never shields a matched student from a stronger eligible claimant. Sections~\ref{sec:order} and~\ref{sec:paodr-limits} state the cost. The number of seats de-reserved depends on the processing order and is not minimal (Example~\ref{ex:order}), although minimizing it is tractable for boundedly many categories (Proposition~\ref{prop:minderes}), and filling the open category by score alone loses both no justified envy and strategy-proofness (Example~\ref{ex:paodr-envy}, Proposition~\ref{prop:paodr-notsp}).

\subsection{Feasibility Graph and Preference-Aware Lexicographic Matching}
\label{sec:preflex}

For a fixed open cutoff $e$, let $G(e)$ be the bipartite graph with left side the students not assigned to the open category, right side $T$, and an edge $(i,t)$ iff $t\in\T(i)$ and $\sigma(i)+k\ge e-\delta$. Under a common bonus a student eligible for several categories is score-admissible at $e$ for all of them or none, yet the graph is still needed, because one-to-one counting forces a choice of which she fills. Write $P(e)$ for the edge set, the \emph{admissible pairs}, and fix an injective index $p:P(e)\to\{1,\dots,|P(e)|\}$ from fixed linear orders on $I$ and $T$. Among capacitated matchings on $G(e)$, \textsc{PrefLexMaxMatching} is the one selected by, in order: (1)~maximize the number of matched pairs; (2)~lexicographically maximize the weakly decreasingly sorted vector of matched elevated scores $\sigma_t(i)=\sigma(i)+k$; (3)~lexicographically minimize the weakly decreasingly sorted vector of preference ranks $r_i(t)$; (4)~minimize $\sum_{(i,t)\in\mu}2^{p(i,t)}$.

Criterion 3 compares matchings by worst preference rank first, then second-worst, so it is egalitarian. Criterion 4 is a pure tie-breaker with no normative content, and it is needed: if two students are eligible for the same two unit-capacity categories and rank them alike, the two assignments have identical cardinality, score and rank vectors. Since distinct edge sets give distinct sums of distinct powers of two, it always resolves such ties, so \textsc{PrefLexMaxMatching} is well defined. The division of labour is sharper than it looks: criteria 1--2 already pin down \emph{who} is admitted, leaving 3--4 to decide only \emph{where}.

\begin{lemma}[Criteria 1 and 2 determine the admitted set]
\label{lem:matroid}
Fix $e$ and right-side capacities $(\hat q^t)_{t\in T}$. All capacitated matchings on $G(e)$ satisfying criteria 1 and 2 match exactly the same set of students; criteria 3 and 4 only choose its assignment to categories.
\end{lemma}

The proof, in Appendix~\ref{app:proofs}, replaces each category by parallel copies and reads criterion 1 as restricting to the bases of the induced transversal matroid, on which criterion 2 is the greedy algorithm and hence deterministic. So criterion 2 fixes the admitted score profile and criterion 3 distributes overlapping students: preferences matter only after size and score are optimized. The matroid structure is the one underlying \cite{Flei03a} and the substitutable choice functions of \cite{GKK+17a,KoSo16a}; what is specific here is that the ground set is defined by an endogenous cutoff.

\textsc{PrefLexMaxMatching} is computable in polynomial time. The four criteria can be collapsed into a single exponentially scaled weight per admissible pair, so that one maximum-weight capacitated bipartite matching realizes them in order, with weights of $O(|I||T|)$ bits and hence a polynomial subproblem, for instance by min-cost flow; Remark~\ref{rem:computation} in Appendix~\ref{app:computation} gives the construction. That form certifies polynomiality but is heavy, and by Lemma~\ref{lem:matroid} an implementation may instead compute the admitted set greedily and then solve a smaller min-cost assignment.

The mechanism itself is Algorithm~\ref{alg:paodr}.

\begin{algorithm}[t]
\caption{Preference-Aware Overlapping De-Reservation (PA-ODR)}
\label{alg:paodr}
\begin{algorithmic}[1]
\Require $I$, scores $\sigma$, eligibility $\T(\cdot)$, preferences $(\succ_i)_{i\in I}$, capacities $q^o,(q^t)_{t\in T}$, bonus $k$, gap $\delta$, a fixed linear order $\prec$ on $T$
\Ensure Matching $\mu=(\mu_o,(\mu_t)_{t\in T})$
\State $\hat q^o\gets q^o$ and $\hat q^t\gets q^t$ for all $t\in T$
\Loop
    \State Assign the top $\min\{\hat q^o,|I|\}$ raw-score students to $o$; call this set $\mu_o$
    \State $e\gets\min_{i\in\mu_o}\sigma(i)$ if $\mu_o\neq\emptyset$, else $e\gets-\infty$
    \State Build $G(e)$ with edge $(i,t)$ iff $t\in\T(i)$ and $\sigma(i)+k\ge e-\delta$, giving $t$ capacity $\hat q^t$
    \State Compute $(\mu_t)_{t\in T}$ by \textsc{PrefLexMaxMatching}
    \If{there exists $t$ with $|\mu_t|<\hat q^t$}
        \State Let $t^\ast$ be the $\prec$-first such category
        \State $\hat q^{t^\ast}\gets\hat q^{t^\ast}-1$ and $\hat q^o\gets\hat q^o+1$
    \Else
        \State \Return $\mu=(\mu_o,(\mu_t)_{t\in T})$
    \EndIf
\EndLoop
\end{algorithmic}
\end{algorithm}

PA-ODR treats score-feasibility as a hard institutional constraint and should be read as a fallback mechanism, not as a quota-preserving reserve rule. The open category is filled first by raw score; reserve assignment runs only over pairs feasible at the induced cutoff; capacity a reserve category cannot fill is de-reserved to the open category. The mechanism thus preserves merit ordering in the open category and uses preferences only to break ties among already-feasible reserve placements, but may change the effective quotas. The order $\prec$ is part of its specification and makes the output well defined (Section~\ref{sec:order}). Note that PA-ODR is preference-aware only on the reserve side: a student strictly preferring a reserve category to $o$ may still be placed in $o$, which Section~\ref{sec:paodr-limits} shows is what costs it both envy-freeness and strategy-proofness.

One feature of the specification deserves flagging here, since every result below is relative to it. The stopping rule is stronger than score-feasibility alone requires: PA-ODR halts only when $|\mu_t|=\hat q^t$ for every $t$, so it de-reserves whenever a reserve seat cannot be filled, not merely when the score rule would otherwise fail. An idle-seat variant, halting when no further edge can be added, is also well defined and score-feasible, retires fewer quotas and matches fewer students; Remark~\ref{sec:trigger} in Appendix~\ref{app:trigger} works both out on the instance of Proposition~\ref{prop:spse-not-sf} and explains why we do not pursue it.

\begin{lemma}[The open cutoff falls monotonically]
\label{lem:monotone}
If $e^{(1)},e^{(2)},\dots$ are the open cutoffs of successive iterations, then $e^{(1)}\ge e^{(2)}\ge\cdots$ and $\mu_o^{(1)}\subseteq\mu_o^{(2)}\subseteq\cdots$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Each non-terminating iteration raises $\hat q^o$ by one and leaves $\sigma$ unchanged. As $\mu_o$ is always the top $\min\{\hat q^o,|I|\}$ students by raw score, these sets are nested in $\hat q^o$, and $e$ is the minimum over a weakly larger set.
\end{proof}

Two opposing forces follow. Lowering $e$ relaxes $\sigma(i)+k\ge e-\delta$, so edges are only added to $G(e)$; but the same step moves one more student into $\mu_o$, so left vertices are only removed. Neither dominates, which is why a single de-reservation can fail to help, as the second branch of Example~\ref{ex:order} shows.

\subsection{Order Dependence and Non-Minimality}
\label{sec:order}

De-reservation enables otherwise infeasible reserve assignment but destroys reserve capacity, so one naturally asks to de-reserve as few seats as possible. PA-ODR fails this, since each decision changes both the cutoff and the remaining pool.

Example~\ref{ex:order} in Appendix~\ref{app:order} shows how much turns on $\prec$. Three students and two unit-capacity reserve categories are arranged so that no reserve seat is admissible at the initial cutoff; de-reserving the first category admits the remaining student through the second and stops after one de-reservation, whereas de-reserving the second absorbs into the open category the only student eligible for the first, so a second de-reservation follows. Both runs match all three students, so the difference is institutional rather than one of coverage: under one order a reserve quota survives and a student enters through it, under the other both quotas are destroyed and she enters through $o$. Since admitting students by the reserve route is the point of a reserve system, this is substantive, and PA-ODR does not minimize de-reservation. Unlike Section~\ref{sec:spse}, the phenomenon is not overlap-driven and we do not present it as such: no student in that example is eligible for two categories. It is the cutoff feedback of Lemma~\ref{lem:monotone} and would arise under single eligibility too; overlap worsens rather than causes it, since an absorbed overlapping student can empty several categories at once.

We therefore state PA-ODR's properties relative to the capacity vector it produces. The minimization problem need not be left open, though: asking for the capacity vector that is realizable, in the sense that every surviving quota can be filled exactly at the cutoff it induces, and that de-reserves as little as possible, defines the problem \textsc{MinDeres}, which Appendix~\ref{app:minderes} states precisely and solves by enumeration in time polynomial for bounded $|T|$ (Proposition~\ref{prop:minderes}). Whether it is fixed-parameter tractable in $|T|$, or NP-hard for unbounded $|T|$, is open. What matters here is that PA-ODR's order dependence is a property of its greedy schedule, not an intractability of the objective.

\subsection{Properties of PA-ODR}

With de-reservation in the model, feasibility and non-wastefulness must be judged against the final vector $\hat q$ rather than the initial $q$; by Example~\ref{ex:order} this qualifier is a genuine weakening, not a bookkeeping convention.

\begin{definition}[Non-wastefulness relative to the final effective capacities]
\label{def:nw-hat}
Let $\hat q$ be the final effective capacity vector produced by PA-ODR. A matching $\mu$ is \emph{non-wasteful relative to $\hat q$} if there are no unmatched $i$ and $c\in V_i$ such that adding $i$ to $c$ yields a matching feasible with respect to $\hat q$ and score-feasible.
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}[Termination, running time, feasibility, score-feasibility, non-wastefulness]
\label{prop:paodr-basic}
PA-ODR terminates; after replacing each $q^c$ by $\min\{q^c,|I|\}$ it runs in time polynomial in the input size. Its output is feasible with respect to the final effective capacities, score-feasible, and non-wasteful relative to them.
\end{proposition}

The proof is in Appendix~\ref{app:proofs}. Termination holds because each de-reservation lowers $\sum_t\hat q^t$ by one; score-feasibility because every reserve assignee is chosen along an edge of $G(e)$ and so clears $e-\delta$ by construction; non-wastefulness because the open category holds the top students and, at termination, every surviving quota is exactly filled. Polynomiality needs the normalization $q^c\gets\min\{q^c,|I|\}$, without which the iteration count is exponential in a binary encoding of the capacities.

\begin{proposition}[Within-category fairness]
\label{prop:paodr-fair}
The output of PA-ODR is fair within categories in the sense of Definition~\ref{def:fair}.
\end{proposition}

The proof is in Appendix~\ref{app:proofs}, and its crucial step is worth stating here: a student who outranks a matched student inside a category is automatically admissible at the prevailing cutoff, since her rival's admissibility already certifies the bound. So the cutoff never shields a matched student from a stronger eligible claimant, and swapping the two would give a matching of equal cardinality and lexicographically larger score vector, contradicting criteria 1 and 2 of \textsc{PrefLexMaxMatching}.

The qualifier ``with respect to the final capacity vector'' attaches to Proposition~\ref{prop:paodr-basic} but not to this one: PA-ODR satisfies the same unweakened within-category fairness as SPSE, needing no de-reservation-adjusted version. The reason is Lemma~\ref{lem:fair-sf} with the first step above---an upward within-category claim can neither break score-feasibility nor be blocked by the cutoff. Unlike SPSE, though, the property comes not from rejection monotonicity but from optimality of the chosen matching inside the final feasibility graph.

\subsection{What PA-ODR Gives Up}
\label{sec:paodr-limits}

Enforcing score-feasibility is not free; the price falls on the incentive and envy side. Both failures share a source: PA-ODR fills the open category by raw score without consulting preferences, which matter only as a tie-breaker.

Example~\ref{ex:paodr-envy} in Appendix~\ref{app:envy} exhibits the envy: with one reserve category and two students who both prefer it to the open category, PA-ODR sends the stronger one to $o$ by raw score and leaves the reserve seat to the weaker, so the stronger has justified envy and Definition~\ref{def:nje} fails. Two features of that example matter. The score rule does not excuse the failure, since honouring the claim is itself score-feasible, so the envy survives even the score-relative reading discussed in Section~\ref{sec:model}; and SPSE returns exactly the alternative assignment there. The two mechanisms differ purely in whose claim they honour, with no feasibility excuse for either.

\begin{proposition}[PA-ODR is not strategy-proof]
\label{prop:paodr-notsp}
There is an instance in which a student obtains a strictly $\succ_i$-better assignment under PA-ODR by misreporting her category preference.
\end{proposition}

The instance is in Appendix~\ref{app:proofs}. Two overlapping students of nearly equal score compete for two unit-capacity categories, both clear the cutoff, so criteria 1 and 2 are silent and criterion 3 decides which of the two admissible routings is chosen. Reporting her second choice as worse than the open category makes the routing that gives it to her look worse under the min--max comparison, and flips the choice to the one she prefers.

This is the manipulation any egalitarian tie-breaker invites: reporting a category as worse than it is makes every assignment giving it to her look worse, which under a min--max rule pays whenever she would otherwise be sacrificed. It is not an artifact of criterion 3 but reflects that PA-ODR is not a deferred-acceptance mechanism---and by Proposition~\ref{prop:impossibility} cannot simply be replaced by one holding capacities fixed.

