\section{Simulations}
\label{sec:sim}

The results so far say that SPSE and PA-ODR fail different requirements, but not how often, nor how much reserve capacity enforcing the score rule actually costs. We therefore implemented both mechanisms in Python and ran them on randomly generated instances, with two purposes: to check that the implementations behave as the proofs predict, and to quantify the trade-off on instances nobody chose adversarially. The natural parameter to vary is the strictness of the rule itself, since $\delta$ is the one quantity a court or a ministry actually sets.

Each instance has $|I|=30$ students with raw scores drawn uniformly from $[0,100]$, $|T|=4$ reserve categories with capacities drawn uniformly from $\{1,\dots,3\}$ and an open capacity from $\{1,\dots,6\}$, giving a mean total reserve capacity of $8.25$ seats, and eligibility drawn so that overlap arises on its own; the bonus is shared by all categories, as the common-bonus model requires. Appendix~\ref{app:simdesign} gives the generator in full. Table~\ref{tab:prototype-time} reports $20$ instances per value of $\delta$ at seed $100$.

