Four things are visible. First, SPSE's failure of score-feasibility is not a corner case built by hand: under the strictest rule it satisfies the score bound on $2$ of $20$ instances, and only as $\delta$ grows to $40$, at which point the constraint is close to vacuous on scores spread over $[0,100]$, does it usually comply. Second, PA-ODR satisfies the bound on all $100$ instances, as Proposition~\ref{prop:paodr-basic} requires, and both mechanisms are fair within categories throughout, as Propositions~\ref{prop:spse-nje} and~\ref{prop:paodr-fair} require. Third, the price is paid in reserve seats and it tracks $\delta$ monotonically: PA-ODR retires $5.75$ of the $8.25$ reserve seats when $\delta=2$ but only $0.10$ when $\delta=40$. So the cost of the cutoff rule is not a fixed overhead of the mechanism but a function of how strict the rule is, and the two mechanisms are close substitutes exactly where the rule is loose. Fourth, the number of students matched is the same for both mechanisms in every row, $11.2$ on average: because de-reservation moves a seat rather than destroying it and students are plentiful, what changes is the route by which a student is admitted, not whether she is.

Two caveats. The PA-ODR times are those of a prototype that enumerates the reserve-side matchings, so their growth in $\delta$ is a property of the prototype, not of the mechanism, whose polynomial implementation is Remark~\ref{rem:computation}. And $20$ instances per row at a single seed are a sanity check on the theory, not evidence about average-case behaviour; varying the degree of overlap and the bonus $k$ as systematically as we vary $\delta$ is left to future work.

\section{Conclusion}

Overlapping reserve categories create a genuine allocation problem because admitted students count toward exactly one category. Score constraints make feasibility depend on endogenous cutoffs, so non-wastefulness must be read relative to the score rule, and full capacity utilization can fail to exist even when every quota is individually honourable. Within-category fairness survives unchanged by Lemma~\ref{lem:fair-sf}, while no justified envy sits between the two, a score-relative weakening of it having content though no result here turns on the reading.

\advisor{Move the table to the second or third page --- done: Table~\ref{tab:comparison} now sits in the Introduction, right after the list of contributions.}

SPSE keeps the initial capacities and inherits the incentive and no-envy properties of deferred acceptance but need not be score-feasible. PA-ODR enforces the score constraint by filling the open category first and converting unfulfillable reserve capacity into open capacity, retaining unweakened within-category fairness but losing strategy-proofness and envy-freeness, and retiring reserve seats where an idle seat would have sufficed (Table~\ref{tab:comparison}). The design tension is thus between fixed reserve capacities and score-feasibility: by Proposition~\ref{prop:impossibility} the latter cannot be bought merely by choosing a better mechanism with capacities held fixed. What it costs is not a fixed overhead either. Section~\ref{sec:sim} finds the capacity PA-ODR must retire falling monotonically as the cutoff rule is relaxed, so the two mechanisms are close substitutes precisely where the rule does not bite, and differ most where it does.

We should not overstate what overlap explains, since both the cutoff-gap breach and the order dependence are visible without it. The specifically overlapping content is narrower and sharper: the routing of a multiply eligible student is an allocation decision that can by itself decide score-feasibility, and the joint-versus-individual gap of Proposition~\ref{prop:impossibility} is a Hall-type obstruction (Remark~\ref{rem:hall}) vanishing without overlap.

